Date:   Fri Nov 20 17:56:57 2026 +0000

    The code generator keeps a local variable's binding in its declaration's body only, so a closure after an if arm captures no unset slot of the arm

    Compiled against the one library, every program died as the library's main class loaded, 'VerifyError: Bad local variable type' in `simplestRationalBetween` (row 1078). `CodeGen.forLocalVarDecl` added each binding to the environment of the code generator that visited the declaration, which `forIf` and the other block forms reuse for an arm's elements, so a binding first in an `if` arm stayed after the arm, and a later local function whose body named a variable of that name captured the arm's slot, unset on the path that skipped the arm. The binding now joins the environment of the declaration's body only, after its initializer; with it, the planner's rehearsal tree runs `Hello` against the one library with the verifier on. Climb batch 34, rung L, with its skeptic's test cases of a nested `do` block's and an initializer block's binding.

